mc_snooping: do not undo a pull that never happened

mc_ipv6_rcv() clones the skb, then tests that the ICMPv6 header is
present before pulling to it:

	err = -EINVAL;
	if (unlikely(!pskb_may_pull(skb2, offset + sizeof(*icmp6h))))
		goto out;

	__skb_pull(skb2, offset);

but the out: label is the common exit and unconditionally runs the undo:

	out:
		__skb_push(skb2, offset);

so on that one early return the push has no matching pull. Every other
goto in the function is below the pull and is correctly paired; this is
the only one above it.

offset comes from ipv6_skip_exthdr(), which advances by each extension
header's own length field and, once it reaches a header it does not
recognise, returns that offset without testing it against skb->len. So a
frame carrying a hop-by-hop header whose length byte claims 200 yields
offset 1648 on a 124-byte frame - the pskb_may_pull() correctly refuses
it, and the undo then walks skb2->data 1632 bytes below skb2->head and
sets skb2->len to 1756 against a 124-byte buffer.

Nothing between that goto and kfree_skb() dereferences skb2->data, and
the free path works off ->head and ->end, so the shipped kernel does not
fault today - the geometry is corrupt but unread. __skb_push() is the
unchecked variant, so nothing catches it either.

One unauthenticated frame from any bridged LAN or Wi-Fi station reaches
it, in the default configuration and with no prior state, which is why it
is worth closing even though it is currently latent. Split the label so
the not-yet-pulled exit skips the undo and still frees the clone.

Found by a coverage-guided ASan harness over mc_rcv() with skb geometry
checked at kfree_skb().

Signed-off-by: Julius Bairaktaris <julius@bairaktaris.de>

--- a/mc_snooping.c
+++ b/mc_snooping.c
@@ -2478,7 +2478,7 @@
 
 	err = -EINVAL;
 	if (unlikely(!pskb_may_pull(skb2, offset + sizeof(*icmp6h))))
-		goto out;
+		goto out_nopull;
 
 	__skb_pull(skb2, offset);
 	skb_reset_transport_header(skb2);
@@ -2585,6 +2585,7 @@
 
 out:
 	__skb_push(skb2, offset);
+out_nopull:
 	if (skb2 != skb)
 		kfree_skb(skb2);
 	return err;
